  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.29.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.29.0 fixes a defect reported from real use rather than found by a suite, and it reaches every agent this distribution ships. A sub-agent could not run bash with a working directory outside the project worktree, because the external_directory permission opened at deny for every sub-agent. Two facts from the runtime source explain it: its bash tool asks under that permission for its working directory, so granting a tool is not granting the paths it can be pointed at; and outside is measured against the git toplevel of wherever the runtime started, with no consultation of the worktree list, so a linked worktree of the very same repository counts as external. The deny was justified on the grounds that a sub-agent has nobody to prompt and that a person approval would outrank it anyway. The first is wrong, and the second is true in the letter and useless in fact, because a config deny returns its error before any prompt is ever published: the approval would have to already exist, granted by another session that asked about the same path, and for a directory only a sub-agent touches nobody ever asks. The baseline is now ask for every agent, and a new command, pegasus directory grant, records the directories a person declares so they survive a restart, since runtime approvals live in memory. One consequence worth carrying into this distribution deliberately: with the baseline at ask, the runtime own auto-approval flags now reach this permission, which they could not while the deny short-circuited the request before publication."
  }
}
